1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,580
Think about your school.

2
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:03,610
Who decided when recess is?

3
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:05,600
Who decided what is for lunch?

4
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:07,230
Not you, probably.

5
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,090
Somebody decided for you.

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:12,530
A whole country works the same way, only much bigger.

7
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:15,117
Nobody votes on every single question.

8
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:18,437
Instead, people pick other people to decide for them.

9
00:00:18,987 --> 00:00:21,691
Picking those deciders is what an election is for.

10
00:00:23,091 --> 00:00:26,681
When people think about the government, they usually picture the President.

11
00:00:27,191 --> 00:00:29,321
But one person does not make the laws.

12
00:00:29,771 --> 00:00:31,371
Laws are made by Congress.

13
00:00:31,921 --> 00:00:35,004
Congress is a big group of people who work in two rooms.

14
00:00:35,454 --> 00:00:39,094
One room is called the House, with four hundred thirty five members.

15
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:42,144
The other room is the Senate, with one hundred.

16
00:00:42,694 --> 00:00:46,109
For an idea to become a law, both rooms have to say yes.

17
00:00:47,509 --> 00:00:48,899
Who gets to be in those rooms?

18
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,139
Every state sends two senators.

19
00:00:51,589 --> 00:00:54,059
Two for the biggest state, two for the smallest.

20
00:00:54,509 --> 00:00:55,709
The House works differently.

21
00:00:56,199 --> 00:00:58,239
States with more people get more seats.

22
00:00:58,739 --> 00:01:00,319
California has fifty two.

23
00:01:00,769 --> 00:01:01,837
Wyoming has one.

24
00:01:02,287 --> 00:01:07,180
So the Senate is about which state you live in, and the House is about how many of you there are.

25
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:10,810
Now, where does that word midterm come from?

26
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,610
A President is elected for four years.

27
00:01:14,110 --> 00:01:18,565
But the whole House is elected every two years, and about a third of the Senate too.

28
00:01:19,015 --> 00:01:22,745
So there is always an election right in the middle of a President's four years.

29
00:01:23,235 --> 00:01:25,195
The middle of the term.

30
00:01:25,645 --> 00:01:27,115
The midterm.

31
00:01:27,565 --> 00:01:28,365
No President is on the ballot that day.

32
00:01:28,565 --> 00:01:29,488
Congress is.

33
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:33,158
A midterm ballot is longer than you would think.

34
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:41,758
In twenty twenty six, all four hundred thirty five House seats were up, plus thirty three Senate seats, plus the governor in thirty six states.

35
00:01:42,278 --> 00:01:45,638
And underneath those, the ones almost nobody talks about.

36
00:01:46,188 --> 00:01:47,236
State lawmakers.

37
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:48,486
Mayors. Judges.

38
00:01:48,756 --> 00:01:50,386
The school board.

39
00:01:50,896 --> 00:01:53,765
The school board is the one that decides things about your school.

40
00:01:55,165 --> 00:01:56,645
So why does it matter who wins?

41
00:01:57,095 --> 00:01:58,355
Here is the first reason.

42
00:01:58,885 --> 00:02:01,945
Congress writes the laws, and a law is for everybody.

43
00:02:02,395 --> 00:02:03,815
And Congress holds the money.

44
00:02:04,365 --> 00:02:08,235
The Constitution says money can only leave the treasury if Congress says so.

45
00:02:08,735 --> 00:02:09,845
Roads.

46
00:02:10,295 --> 00:02:11,095
Parks.

47
00:02:10,925 --> 00:02:11,900
School lunches.

48
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:13,150
The army.

49
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:17,504
Somebody has to decide how much goes where, and that somebody is Congress.

50
00:02:18,904 --> 00:02:20,264
Here is the second reason.

51
00:02:20,764 --> 00:02:26,044
A midterm is the country's chance to say, halfway through, keep going, or slow down.

52
00:02:26,544 --> 00:02:28,464
And people really do use it that way.

53
00:02:29,014 --> 00:02:34,183
In the last twenty three midterms, the President's party lost seats in the House twenty times.

54
00:02:34,633 --> 00:02:36,663
It only gained seats three times.

55
00:02:37,143 --> 00:02:42,023
So a midterm can change what a President is able to get done, without changing the President.

56
00:02:43,423 --> 00:02:45,003
And here is the strange part.

57
00:02:45,453 --> 00:02:47,233
Fewer people vote in midterms.

58
00:02:47,763 --> 00:02:52,733
In the last Presidential election, about two out of every three grown ups who could vote did.

59
00:02:53,283 --> 00:02:55,553
In the last midterm, fewer than half.

60
00:02:56,073 --> 00:02:59,084
Meanwhile, tiny numbers decide real races.

61
00:02:59,534 --> 00:03:04,064
In twenty seventeen, a state election in Virginia ended in a perfect tie.

62
00:03:04,594 --> 00:03:06,894
Eleven thousand six hundred eight votes each.

63
00:03:07,414 --> 00:03:09,287
The winner's name was pulled out of a bowl.

64
00:03:10,687 --> 00:03:12,737
You cannot vote until you are eighteen.

65
00:03:13,197 --> 00:03:14,627
That does not leave you out of it.

66
00:03:15,077 --> 00:03:17,677
You can find out who represents your neighborhood, by name.

67
00:03:18,227 --> 00:03:20,479
You can go along with a grown up when they vote.

68
00:03:20,929 --> 00:03:24,759
You can watch what the school board decides, because that one is about you.

69
00:03:25,299 --> 00:03:27,949
And you can ask the grown ups in your house one question.

70
00:03:28,489 --> 00:03:29,289
Did you vote?

71
00:03:30,522 --> 00:03:34,112
So, a midterm is the election in the middle of a President's term.

72
00:03:34,662 --> 00:03:38,902
The whole House, a third of the Senate, and a long list of local jobs.

73
00:03:39,382 --> 00:03:42,571
Those people write the laws and decide where the money goes.

74
00:03:43,021 --> 00:03:44,781
Fewer people show up to choose them.

75
00:03:45,231 --> 00:03:47,646
Which means the ones who do show up, decide.

76
00:03:50,446 --> 00:03:51,356
One last thing.

77
00:03:51,806 --> 00:03:53,576
At the beginning we asked a question.

78
00:03:54,126 --> 00:03:55,146
Who gets to decide?

79
00:03:55,596 --> 00:03:56,986
Here is the real answer.

80
00:03:57,436 --> 00:03:59,096
Somebody decides everything.

81
00:03:59,546 --> 00:04:00,576
When recess is.

82
00:04:01,026 --> 00:04:01,896
What is for lunch.

83
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:03,236
Where the money goes.

84
00:04:03,686 --> 00:04:05,288
The only question is ever who.

85
00:04:05,738 --> 00:04:07,868
Right now, it is the grown ups who show up.

86
00:04:08,388 --> 00:04:09,988
In a few years, it will be you.

87
00:04:10,438 --> 00:04:12,078
Not in some big dramatic way.

88
00:04:12,618 --> 00:04:16,608
Just a line, a curtain, a piece of paper, on a Tuesday in November.

89
00:04:17,058 --> 00:04:20,538
And on that Tuesday, the people who show up are the people who decide.

90
00:04:21,018 --> 00:04:22,950
So when it is your turn, show up.
